## STILL MISSING

### No CARDINALITY, so |G| = [G:H]·|H| is a statement about LISTINGS

Judson's Lagrange is an equation between numbers: `|G|/|H| = [G:H]`. 2b4m has no
cardinality function on a sort, so the whole chapter is phrased with LISTINGS — a
sequence whose entries below a length are pairwise distinct and exhaust what they
list (the idiom from `std/permutation/count.b4m`). `std/group/lagrange.b4m` therefore
proves:

> k coset representatives + a p-long listing of H  ⟹  a (k·p)-long listing of G.

That is the mathematical content, and it is what the corollaries actually consume. But
it is NOT Judson's sentence, and three things are awkward as a result:

- **"the number of distinct left cosets"** has no term. `[G:H]` enters as a HYPOTHESIS
  (you are handed k representatives) rather than being DEFINED from the group and the
  subgroup. Nothing stops a caller supplying a k that is wrong; the theorem is still
  true, it just says less than Judson's.
- **Divisibility needs a bridge.** `|H| divides |G|` is not a restatement of the
  listing theorem — it needs "G is listed by n" + "G is listed by k·p" ⟹ n = k·p
  (listing-length uniqueness) and then `p | n` from the product. The uniqueness half
  is the load-bearing piece and is not currently stated for group listings.
- **Corollary chains multiply.** `[G:K] = [G:H][H:K]` is division arithmetic in
  Judson; as listings it is a three-way block decomposition, i.e. a second instance of
  the whole Lagrange construction rather than two divisions.

Not a defect — the listing idiom is a deliberate choice and it carries the content.
But it means each §6.2 corollary is its own piece of work, not a one-liner off the
theorem, and the chapter should say so rather than imply the corollaries are free.

### A listing's length is not known to be unique (for groups)

Needed by every divisibility corollary. For PERMUTATIONS this exists —
`std/permutation/count.b4m` has the pigeonhole machinery and
`listingsInBijectionHaveEqualLengths` (~472 lines) — but it hardcodes `compose` and
the permutation sort. Generalizing it needs either sort polymorphism (absent) or a
re-proof over the group sort. See the next entry: this is the same wall.

### `Seq` is re-postulated per carrier — five copies and counting

`std/sequence.b4m` has the abstract fold, but each concrete sequence theory must
RESTATE the sequence-EXISTENCE postulates over its own carrier, because a model
transfers THEOREMS and must DISCHARGE axioms — and `seqSingleton`/`seqConcat`/… are
axioms. So `std/group/sequence.b4m`, `std/integer/sequence.b4m`,
`std/permutation/sequence.b4m`, `std/integer/mod-n-listing.b4m` and
`std/set/subsets.b4m` each carry their own copy, each with a comment explaining why.

This is what FIXED THE BASE SORT for Chapter 6. `std/group/sequence.b4m` postulates
`Seq`/`at` over `std/group.b4m`'s `Grp`, so anything that counts group elements must
live over THAT sort — the listing layer chose the carrier, and `std/group/coset.b4m`
had to follow it (not the other way round). Recorded in memory as
`abstract-collections-direction`: the fix is a container theory parameterized by its
carrier, so a sequence over any sort is one model away instead of five re-postulated
axiom sets. Until then, every new counting theory pays the copy.

### Two same-named sorts from different origins print identically

`expected sort 'Grp', got 'Grp'` — the diagnostic names NEITHER origin. Hit while
importing `std/subgroup.b4m` (which then declared its own `Grp`) as a base for
cosets. It cost a full detour: I read the message as an obstacle to route around,
concluded the FILE was wrong, and wrote a commit message blaming it, when the message
was really saying "these are two different sorts that happen to share a name".

Less likely to fire in std now that `subgroup` aliases (`1b8b87a`), but the
diagnostic is unchanged and will catch the next person. Printing the origin file for
each side is a cheap fix.

### `--axioms` lists an axiom per ORIGIN, and that reads like a bug

When `std/group/coset.b4m` declared its own copies of the three subgroup criteria and
reached `std/subgroup.b4m` through a model, `--axioms` listed each criterion TWICE —
once per site. I wrote this off as "the ledger double-reporting a model transfer,"
compared it against `tests/cases/model_subgroup_transfer.b4m` (which shows the same
shape, for the same reason), and moved on.

It was not double-reporting. It was correctly reporting TWO ORIGINS — which is exactly
the duplication I should have removed. Aliasing collapsed it to one entry each.

**Lesson for the next reader of this file:** a repeated axiom NAME at two sites in
`--axioms` means two declarations, never one fact counted twice. The ledger is right;
check the design.

### `2b4m fmt` CRASHES on an unbalanced proof brace

```
thread N panic: integer overflow
src/fmt.zig:191:43 in render     if (in_proof) extra_depth -= 1;
```

`extra_depth` is unsigned and decremented past zero. One too many `}` in a proof
core-dumps the formatter instead of diagnosing, which violates the repo's
hard-errors-not-warnings rule and tells the author nothing about WHERE. `check`
diagnoses the same file fine, so the workaround is to run `check` first; the useful
locator in the meantime is a one-liner printing running brace depth at each `qed`
(a nonzero value names the offending theorem).

### `case` is a step's JUSTIFICATION, not a step of its own

Minor, but it cost two cycles. `case <disj> { … }` goes in the justification slot of
the step that states the case-split's CONCLUSION:

```2b4m
@conclusion-indices-equal |
  i = j
  case i-splits {
    @when-i-is-low | assume … { … }
    @when-i-is-high | assume … { … }
  }
```

Writing it as a bare `@by-case | case i-splits { … }` step is a parse error
("expected an expression, got 'case'"). The cheatsheet shows the form inline but not
that the conclusion formula must be on the step carrying it.

### The index-arithmetic lemmas live in the wrong file

`indexSplits`, `belowTheBaseIsBelowTheSum` and `indexPastTheBase` are pure
Peano-order facts — "an index below base+m is either below base or is base+j for some
j<m" — with NO permutation content, but they are declared in
`std/permutation/count.b4m`. Three unrelated files now alias them out of there:
`std/permutation/dihedral.b4m`, `std/integer/mod-n-generators.b4m`, and now
`std/group/lagrange.b4m`. Any file doing a block decomposition needs them, and none
of those should import the permutation counting theory to get them. They belong in
`std/peano/order.b4m` (or a `std/peano/block-index.b4m`). Mechanical move.

